  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/equipment/rig-floor-tools" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Equipment — Rig Floor</div>
    <h1>Rig Floor <span>Hand Tools</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">Every connection made or broken on the drill floor runs through the same handful of tools. Here's what each one does, and how a roughneck crew actually uses them together to run pipe.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="what-theyre-for">What They're For</h2>
        <p>Running a string in or out of the hole means making up or breaking out a threaded connection every single stand, hundreds of times over the life of a well. The tools on this page are what a <a href="/careers/roughneck">roughneck</a> crew uses to grip, spin, torque, and support pipe safely through that cycle — none of them do anything on their own; they work in a fixed sequence every time a connection is handled.</p>

        <KeyFacts
          lang="en"
          rows={[["Core tools", "Tongs (or iron roughneck), slips, elevators, spinning wrench or kelly spinner."], ["Primary job", "Grip, spin, torque and support pipe during every connection made up or broken out."], ["Who operates them", "The rig floor crew (roughnecks), under the driller's direction from the console."], ["Manual vs mechanized", "Manual tongs and slips are the traditional setup; an iron roughneck mechanizes make-up/break-out and has become standard on most modern rigs."]]}
        />

        <h2 id="tongs">Tongs</h2>
        <p>Tongs grip the pipe body and apply the rotational torque that makes up or breaks out a threaded connection. Traditional rig floors use two sets — a pair of manual tongs, one holding the lower joint stationary while the other applies torque to the upper joint — operated by hand with the crew pulling a tong line — a cable wrapped around the cathead, a rotating spool on the drawworks that supplies the pulling force — for leverage. This is physically demanding, repetitive work, and it's one of the main reasons a roughneck's early months are as much about building the specific strength and technique this job requires as learning the equipment itself.</p>
        <p>An <strong>iron roughneck</strong> mechanizes the same job: a single hydraulically powered unit that spins the pipe up and then applies precise, measured torque to the exact value the connection calls for, without a crew member physically pulling tong lines. It's become the standard on most modern land and offshore rigs because it's both faster and safer — torque is applied consistently to spec rather than by feel, and it removes hands from the highest pinch-point risk area on the floor during make-up and break-out.</p>

        <h2 id="slips">Slips</h2>
        <p>Slips are wedge-shaped, toothed inserts that sit in the rotary table (or in a casing/tubing spider for those strings) and grip the pipe body to hold the full weight of the string hanging in the hole once it's released from the elevators. They work purely on wedging action: the weight of the string pulling down drives the tapered slip segments tighter against the pipe, which means the grip gets stronger exactly when it needs to, under load — not something that can loosen on its own mid-connection.</p>
        <p>Slips are sized to the specific pipe diameter and weight being run, the same way a <a href="/equipment/bop#rams">pipe ram</a> is sized to one pipe diameter — a mismatched slip either won't seat correctly or won't support the load it's rated for, which is a serious handling hazard rather than a minor inconvenience.</p>

        <h2 id="elevators">Elevators</h2>
        <p>Elevators are the hinged, clamping assembly that latches around the pipe just below a tool joint's upset (the thicker shoulder at each pipe end) and connects up to the traveling block via bails, letting the hoisting system lift and lower the entire string. Where slips hold the string stationary in the rotary table, elevators are what actually move it — raising a new stand into position to make up, or lowering the string to set it back in the slips once a connection is broken.</p>
        <p>The handoff between the two is the core rhythm of tripping pipe: elevators lower the string until its weight transfers onto the slips, the connection gets broken or made up, then the elevators latch the next joint and the cycle repeats. Every trip in or out of the hole is this same sequence, run however many times the string is long.</p>

        <h2 id="spinning-wrench">Spinning Wrench and Kelly Spinner</h2>
        <p>Before tongs can apply real torque, a connection has to be spun together by hand-tight engagement first — threading the joint far enough that the tong can then apply the final, measured torque without cross-threading. A <strong>spinning wrench</strong> (or power tong set to spin mode on modern rigs) does this fast, hydraulically powered spin-up, replacing the older method of spinning pipe by hand with a chain tong.</p>
        <p>A <strong>kelly spinner</strong> is a related but distinct tool found on rigs still using a conventional kelly and rotary table rather than a top drive — it's mounted at the top of the kelly itself and spins the kelly into the connection below it. On a <a href="/equipment/rotating-system">top drive rig</a>, the top drive's own motor performs this function directly, which is one of several reasons top drive rigs need fewer separate floor tools than a conventional kelly rig does.</p>

        <h2 id="how-they-work-together">How They Work Together: Making Up a Connection</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>The string already sits in the slips</strong>, set in the rotary table holding its full weight from the previous cycle.</li>
          <li><strong>Elevators lower the new stand</strong>, aligning the pin end with the box end of the stationary joint held in the slips below.</li>
          <li><strong>Spinning wrench or iron roughneck spin tool engages</strong>, threading the connection hand-tight without cross-threading.</li>
          <li><strong>Tongs (or the iron roughneck's torque function) apply final make-up torque</strong> to the exact value specified for that connection — under-torque risks the joint backing off downhole, over-torque risks damaging the threads. The string stays supported in the slips throughout this entire step.</li>
          <li><strong>Elevators take the string's weight</strong> and the slips release, letting the now-extended string lower further into the hole.</li>
          <li><strong>Slips reset around the string</strong> at its new position; elevators release and move up to latch the next stand, and the cycle repeats.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Breaking out a connection while tripping out of the hole runs the identical sequence in reverse. Every one of these tools exists because a connection has to be gripped, spun, torqued, and supported as four genuinely separate mechanical jobs — no single tool on the floor does more than one of them.</p>

        <div class="note-box">
          <strong>Reference only.</strong> Torque specifications, slip ratings, and elevator capacities vary by manufacturer, pipe size, and connection type — always confirm against your rig's actual torque chart and equipment spec sheets before relying on any figure for a real job.
        </div>
      </div>

